  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Perfect Squares, Divisibility, and Nearest Squares",
    "description": "Find the least changes or factors needed to reach a perfect square.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach perfect-square tests through prime factorization: a product is a perfect square exactly when every prime exponent is even. Use this to find the least multiplier or divisor that makes a number square, and combine divisibility requirements using an LCM before correcting prime exponents. For nearest-square problems, locate consecutive squares around the number and compare the required addition or subtraction; handle boundary words such as least, greatest, strictly between, and digit-length constraints carefully. Include greatest/least perfect squares in a range and perfect squares with a required digit count. Learners should show factor exponents or consecutive-square bounds and verify the final number."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A positive integer is a perfect square exactly when every prime exponent is even. Nearest-square questions then become comparisons between consecutive squares, with careful attention to endpoint wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Factor 72=2³×3². One additional factor of 2 makes all exponents even, so the least multiplier is 2 and 72×2=144=12². To find a least divisor instead, remove the unpaired factors and confirm the quotient remains an integer square.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
